`IterTag.render_component` has a fixed control-flow shape determined by the render function’s arity and return type. In the test, the three foreach cases always exercise the same branches: the label lambda has one argument and returns a tuple, so the function takes the one-arg path, skips the `Foreach/Cond` wrapper check, takes the tuple-wrapper branch, then sets the key; the flag lambda has two arguments and returns a `Cond`, so it takes the two-arg path, wraps the `Cond` in a fragment, skips the tuple branch, then sets the key; the nested rows case calls `render_component` twice per outer item in a fixed pattern, with the outer lambda returning a `Foreach` and the inner lambda returning a plain component. Nothing in `render_component` depends on mutable global state except the generated variable names used in values, and those do not change which lines execute. So across identical runs, the sequence of executed lines inside this function remains the same, including repeated visits from nested foreach renderings.

## Answer
{"invariant_exists":true}
